## Web pages {#access-web}

### Check the name {#access-web-dns}

Goal: make sure your computer finds the system's web pages under {{ f.domain }}.

{% set probe = (web | first).host ~ "." ~ f.domain %}
{{ cmds("ping -c 1 " ~ probe, "ping -n 1 " ~ probe) }}
**What you should see:** the address {{ traefik_nodes | map(attribute="address") | join(" or ") }}{{ (" (public: " ~ (traefik_nodes | map(attribute="public_ip") | select | join(", ")) ~ ")") if traefik_nodes | map(attribute="public_ip") | select | list }}. That is the server that answers the web pages. A reply is not needed: some networks block ping, but the address must be right.

{% if dns.get("mode") == "internal" %}
The names come from your company's own DNS (the internet's phone book), with one record for every name under {{ f.domain }}.
{% elif dns.get("mode") == "hosts" %}
There is no DNS for {{ f.domain }}, so you add the names to your computer's hosts file (next step).
{% else %}
The names come from the public DNS (the internet's phone book), with one record for every name under {{ f.domain }}.
{% endif %}

**If it fails:** "could not find host" means the name is not known. {{ "Add the hosts lines below." if dns.get("mode") == "hosts" else "Ask the vendor to check the DNS record." }}

{% if dns.get("mode") == "hosts" %}
### Add the names to the hosts file {#access-web-hosts}

Goal: tell your computer the address of each web page, because there is no DNS. These are the lines from the file hosts.txt:

{% set t = traefik_nodes | first %}
```text
{% for w in web %}
{{ t.public_ip or t.address }}  {{ w.host }}.{{ f.domain }}
{% endfor %}
```

A hosts file cannot use wildcards, so every page has its own line, and each name has one address only: when that server is down, the pages are down for you. A record in your company DNS is the better solution; ask your IT team.

{% if win %}
Windows (PowerShell): open Notepad as administrator, paste the lines at the end, save, then clear the name cache. Company laptops often block this.

```powershell
Start-Process notepad "$env:SystemRoot\System32\drivers\etc\hosts" -Verb RunAs
ipconfig /flushdns
```

{% endif %}
{% if "macos" in oses_shown %}
macOS (Terminal): open the file, paste the lines at the end, save with Ctrl+O and Enter, close with Ctrl+X, then clear the name cache.

```sh
sudo nano /etc/hosts
sudo dscacheutil -flushcache; sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder
```

{% endif %}
{% if "linux" in oses_shown %}
Linux (Terminal): open the file, paste the lines at the end, save with Ctrl+O and Enter, close with Ctrl+X, then clear the name cache (on systems with systemd-resolved).

```sh
sudo nano /etc/hosts
resolvectl flush-caches
```

{% endif %}
**What you should see:** the ping in the step above now shows the right address.

**If it fails:** a browser with "secure DNS" turned on may skip the hosts file *(not tested)*. Turn that setting off in the browser, or ask your IT team.

{% endif %}
### Certificates {#access-web-tls}

{% if f.ca_file or client_ca %}
Goal: let your computer trust the system's certificates (the proof that a page is really the system's). They come from a private certificate authority, so your computer must trust the file **{{ slug }}-ca.crt** from the handover pack. All the commands here are *(not tested)*.

{% if win %}
Windows: double-click {{ slug }}-ca.crt, choose Install Certificate, then put it in "Trusted Root Certification Authorities". Or in PowerShell:

```powershell
certutil -user -addstore Root {{ slug }}-ca.crt
```

Firefox on Windows uses this only when the setting `security.enterprise_roots.enabled` is on (type `about:config` in the address bar).

{% endif %}
{% if "macos" in oses_shown %}
macOS (Terminal):

```sh
security add-trusted-cert -r trustRoot \
  -k ~/Library/Keychains/login.keychain-db {{ slug }}-ca.crt
```

{% endif %}
{% if "linux" in oses_shown %}
Linux (Terminal), on Debian or Ubuntu:

```sh
sudo cp {{ slug }}-ca.crt /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/{{ slug }}.crt
sudo update-ca-certificates
```

On Fedora:

```sh
sudo cp {{ slug }}-ca.crt /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/
sudo update-ca-trust
```

Chrome and Firefox on Linux keep their own list (package libnss3-tools):

```sh
certutil -d sql:$HOME/.pki/nssdb -A -t "C,," -n {{ slug }} -i {{ slug }}-ca.crt
```

{% endif %}
**What you should see:** after a browser restart, the pages open without a warning.

**If it fails:** close every browser window and open it again. If the warning stays, ask the vendor.

{% elif f.tls == "selfsigned" %}
The system has no certificate that your computer can trust (the "selfsigned" setting). The browser warns on every page, and again each time the gateway restarts. This is expected. To continue anyway:

- Chrome and Edge: click Advanced, then Proceed.
- Firefox: click Advanced, then Accept the Risk and Continue.
- Safari: click Show Details, then visit this website.

{% if f.admin_sso == "keycloak" %}
With the Keycloak login, expect a warning on each of oauth.{{ f.domain }}, keycloak.{{ f.domain }} and the page itself.
{% endif %}
The warning is safe to pass only for the addresses in the table below. Recommended: a real certificate (the "custom" setting, or a private ACME server); see the known issues in {{ doc_ref }}.

{% elif f.tls == "custom" %}
The system uses its own certificate{{ (", valid until " ~ f.tls_cert.not_after) if f.tls_cert }}. When it comes from a public authority there is nothing to do. When it comes from your company's own authority, your IT team installs that authority on the laptops.

{% else %}
The certificates come from Let's Encrypt, a public authority. Your browser trusts them already: there is nothing to do.

{% endif %}
