## SSH: the login to the servers {#access-ssh}

This part is for operators. SSH (Secure Shell) is the safe way to type commands on a server. You log in with a key: a pair of files, one private (it stays on your laptop) and one public (it goes to the servers). The vendor's own key is never handed over.

### Check the SSH program {#access-ssh-client}

Goal: make sure your computer has the OpenSSH program.

{{ per_os("ssh -V", "ssh -V", "ssh -V") }}
**What you should see:** a version, for example "OpenSSH_9.6".

**If it fails:** {% if win %}on Windows, install it from PowerShell opened as administrator: `Add-WindowsCapability -Online -Name OpenSSH.Client~~~~0.0.1.0`. {% endif %}{% if "linux" in oses_shown %}On Linux, install it with `sudo apt install openssh-client`. {% endif %}{% if "macos" in oses_shown %}macOS always has it. {% endif %}Use OpenSSH, not PuTTY: PuTTY needs its own key format.

{% if ssh_handover == "generated" %}
### Save your key {#access-ssh-key}

Goal: put the key that the vendor made for you in the right place. It is the attachment {{ slug }}_ed25519 of the entry "{{ groups.infra[first_lang] }}/SSH key (client)" in the credentials file. Save it under this name:

{{ cmds("mkdir -p ~/.ssh\n# save the attachment as ~/.ssh/" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519, then:\nchmod 600 ~/.ssh/" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519", "New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\"\n# save the attachment as $env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\\" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519") }}
**What you should see:** the file {{ slug }}_ed25519 in your .ssh folder.

**If it fails:** if the attachment is missing, ask the vendor.

{% else %}
### Make your own key {#access-ssh-key}

Goal: make your key pair, then send only the public part (the file ending in .pub) to the vendor.

{{ cmds("mkdir -p ~/.ssh\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519 -C \"your name\"", "New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\"\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f $env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\\" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519 -C \"your name\"") }}
Choose a passphrase for the key when asked; it protects the key if your laptop is lost.

**What you should see:** two new files: {{ slug }}_ed25519 (private: never send it) and {{ slug }}_ed25519.pub (public: send this one to the vendor).

**If it fails:** "No such file or directory" means the .ssh folder is missing: run the first line again.

### The vendor adds your key {#access-ssh-add}

Goal: the vendor puts your public key on every server. ansibro does not do this, so the operator does it by hand, once per server, with their own access:

```sh
cat {{ slug }}_ed25519.pub | ssh -p {{ f.ssh.port }} {{ f.ssh.user }}@<server> \
  "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && chmod 700 ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys \
  && chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"
```

`<server>` is each address in the next table. This is a row of the acceptance checklist (Annex 1 of the Handover Certificate).

{% endif %}
### Add the SSH settings {#access-ssh-config}

Goal: give each server a short name, so `ssh {{ mgr }}` is all you type. The file **ssh_config.txt** from the handover pack has the settings; add it to the end of your SSH settings file:

{{ cmds("cat ssh_config.txt >> ~/.ssh/config", "Get-Content .\\ssh_config.txt |\n  Add-Content -Encoding ascii \"$env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\\config\"") }}
It gives these names:

| Name                        | Server          | Address           | Goes through                  |
|:----------------------------|:----------------|:------------------|:------------------------------|
{% for n in reach %}
| `{{ n.alias }}` | {{ n.name }} | {{ n.address }} | {{ ("`" ~ n.jump ~ "`") if n.jump and n.jump != n.alias else "Direct" }} |
{% endfor %}

Every name logs in as {{ f.ssh.user }} on port {{ f.ssh.port }}, with the key ~/.ssh/{{ slug }}_ed25519.
{% if reach | selectattr("jump") | list %}
A server without a public address is reached through another server ("goes through"){{ "; because ssh.allow_from is set, that server's address must also be on the list" if f.ssh.allow_from }}.
{% endif %}
{% if "macos" in oses_shown %}
On macOS you can add two lines under each `Host` line so the key passphrase is kept in the Keychain: `UseKeychain yes` and `AddKeysToAgent yes`.
{% endif %}

**What you should see:** the `Host` lines at the end of the file.

**If it fails:** if you already had a `Host` with the same name, remove the old one.

### First connection {#access-ssh-first}

Goal: log in to the first server once and check that it is the real one.

{{ cmds("ssh " ~ mgr, "ssh " ~ mgr) }}
{% set fps = (check.get("servers") if check else None) or {} %}
The first time, SSH shows the server's fingerprint (a code starting with SHA256:) and asks to continue. Compare it with this table, then type `yes`. If they differ, type `no` and call the vendor.

| Server          | Host-key fingerprint                                                          |
|:----------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
{% for n in reach %}
{% set fp = (fps.get(n.name) or {}).get("fingerprint") %}
| {{ n.name }} | {{ ("`" ~ fp ~ "`") if fp else "Not checked" }} |
{% endfor %}

{% set fpn = namespace(any=false) %}
{% for k in fps %}{% if (fps[k] or {}).get("fingerprint") %}{% set fpn.any = true %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% if not fpn.any %}
The fingerprints were not checked by the acceptance test. Ask the vendor to read them out from each server with `ssh-keygen -lf /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub`.

{% endif %}

**What you should see:** a prompt like `{{ f.ssh.user }}@{{ first.name }}:~$`. Type `exit` to leave. Commands for the system need `sudo` in front, because its files under /opt/ansibro belong to the administrator (root).

**If it fails:**

- "Permission denied (publickey)": your key is not on that server yet, or the key file name is wrong.
- "Connection timed out": your address is not in ssh.allow_from, or the provider's firewall blocks port {{ f.ssh.port }}.
- "UNPROTECTED PRIVATE KEY FILE": the key file is readable by others. Fix it:

{{ cmds("chmod 600 ~/.ssh/" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519", "icacls $env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\\" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519 /inheritance:r `\n  /grant:r \"$($env:USERNAME):(R)\"") }}
### Remember the key passphrase {#access-ssh-agent}

Goal: type the key's passphrase once per session instead of at every login.

{{ per_os("Get-Service ssh-agent | Set-Service -StartupType Automatic\nStart-Service ssh-agent\nssh-add $env:USERPROFILE\\.ssh\\" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519", "ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519", "ssh-add ~/.ssh/" ~ slug ~ "_ed25519") }}
{% if win %}
On Windows, the first two lines need PowerShell opened as administrator.
{% endif %}

**What you should see:** "Identity added".

**If it fails:** "Could not open a connection to your authentication agent" means the agent is not running{{ ": run the Windows lines as administrator" if win }}.

